# kldload.rules — the kldload ruleset for vmxplore.
#
# Read by rules.go when running on a kldload host (or via --rules). This file
# is the ONLY place kldload naming conventions live — the compiled core knows
# none of these strings (docs/VM-CONSOLE-DESIGN.md: "estate grouping and
# snapshot classification are data, not Go"). Edit here, no rebuild needed
# when installed at /etc/vmxplore/rules.
#
# Format (first match wins, order matters):
#   snap  <class>  <name-prefix>     class of a snapshot by its @name prefix;
#                                    anything unmatched is class "human"
#   group <regex>  <label…>          estate group for a domain name; $1…$9
#                                    expand capture groups; unmatched domains
#                                    land in "ungrouped"
#
# Classes the UI understands: noise (collapsed to a count), human (the ✎
# count — what an operator made), golden, provenance, checkpoint, repl.

# sanoid (every 15 min, ~600 per zvol on a real host) and kvm-snapshot.timer —
# pure noise at estate scale; the UI shows only their count.
snap noise      autosnap_
snap noise      auto-
snap human      manual-
snap golden     golden
snap provenance clone-
snap provenance create-
snap checkpoint pre-kube-
snap repl       repl-

# Goldens first so `-golden` names never fall through to a cluster rule.
group ^k8s-golden$              goldens
group ^klab-golden-[a-z]+$      goldens
# klab's desktop lineage (klab-desktop-<distro>) is a golden set too, but its
# name carries the KIND rather than the word "golden", so none of the rules
# above see it and the images landed ungrouped in the estate -- built, usable,
# and invisible under Goldens (operator, 2026-08-15). Matched before the klab
# blue/green rule below, per the ordering this section exists to guarantee.
group ^klab-desktop-[a-z0-9]+$  goldens
# Same story for the OpenZFS test lineage: klab-ztest-<distro> IS a golden
# (klab's golden_name() mints all three kinds), and the klab rule below was
# claiming it first, so the one image the ZFS lab is built from sat under
# "klab" next to the throwaways cloned off it. Deployed ztest VMs carry a
# trailing -<n> and still land in klab, which is where they belong.
group ^klab-ztest-[a-z0-9]+$    goldens

# The OpenZFS test lab gets its OWN group, not "goldens" and not "ungrouped".
#
# kzfs-test names its images kzfstest-golden-<distro>, and NOTHING here matched
# them: the -golden catch-all below wants the name to END in "-golden", and
# these end in the distro. So five running test-lab images sat in the
# ungrouped pile with the throwaways, which is where the operator found them
# (.113, 2026-08-17: "they were added to the uncategorized instead of creating
# a new category for the test images").
#
# A separate group rather than folding them into "goldens" is the point: these
# are a different lab with a different lifecycle — built by kzfs-test, rebuilt
# per OpenZFS release, and torn down between runs — and mixing them with the
# blue/green base images makes both harder to read.
#
# HISTORY: a comment in rules.go asserted that kzfstest-golden-* was "a prefix
# nothing in kldload ever creates" and dropped the kzfs-test tool tile on that
# basis. It was wrong; kzfs-test creates exactly that prefix. Verified against
# five running domains before writing this rule.
group ^kzfstest-golden-[a-z0-9]+$       zfs test lab
group ^kzfstest-[a-z0-9]+-[0-9]+$       zfs test lab
group ^kzfstest-.+$                     zfs test lab

group ^.+-golden$               goldens

# klab blue/green lab pairs: klab-{blue,green,test}-<distro>. A numbered
# ztest clone (klab-ztest-<distro>-<n>) is a lab machine, not the golden
# it came from, so it is matched here and not by the goldens rule above.
group ^klab-(blue|green|test)-[a-z]+$   klab
group ^klab-ztest-[a-z0-9]+-[0-9]+$     klab

# kspawn throwaway clusters: kspawn-<cluster>-<NN>.
group ^kspawn-(.+)-[0-9]+$      kspawn: $1

# k8s clusters from kube-cluster: <cluster>-cp[N] / <cluster>-w<N>; the
# live estate also carries dashed forms (kldload-w-2), hence the -?.
group ^(.+)-cp-?[0-9]*$         k8s: $1
group ^(.+)-w-?[0-9]+$          k8s: $1
